    thinking: "**Analyzing Teal\'s Significance**\n\nI\'ve discovered numerous instances of \"teal\" within `src/App.tsx`. However, a new approach arises with Tailwind v4, which offers the ability to redefine \"teal\" using the `@theme` block. This allows for customization, such as reassigning `--color-teal-50`.\n\n\n**Mapping Teal Colors**\n\nI\'m now focusing on mapping all \"teal\" color shades to a consistent and aesthetically pleasing palette. The goal is to redefine \"teal\" within the application to match the user\'s specific request for `#9fc5e9`. I\'m calculating lighter and darker variations of this base color, resulting in shades from `#f0f6fc` to `#a3c7eb`, to ensure a coherent and high-contrast UI across different components.\n\n\n**Defining Teal Variants**\n\nI\'ve determined suitable shades: `#7fadcf` for 500, `#608fad` for 600, then darker until `950` at `#0b1721`. The focus now shifts to implementation, can the existing classnames like `teal-300` and `teal-400` be redefined? Fortunately, it seems we can leverage Tailwind v4\'s `@theme` block in `src/index.css` for clean, high-performance overrides using CSS variables. Documentation confirms this as the best approach.\n\n\n**Confirming CSS Variable Overrides**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that Tailwind v4\'s color overrides using CSS variables are exactly what I need. Defining `--color-teal-*` within the `@theme` block will dynamically update all associated classes such as `bg-teal-500`, etc. I\'m excited by this elegant and powerful approach. Now I\'m reviewing the existing classes to be overridden. The grep provided a detailed list of usages.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will add the custom `#9fc5e9` color theme overrides to `src/index.css` by overriding the `teal` color palette in the `@theme` block. This is the cleanest, most robust way to re-theme the entire application using CSS variables."
